    "result_text": "The build passes and the test is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain blocklist rejection following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**Change:** When an owner's email is present and syntactically valid but its domain is on the disposable-domain blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`), the create/update pipeline now rejects with **400**.\n\n**Files:**\n- `ValidateOwner.java` \u2014 added a `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` set and, right after the existing syntactic email check, extracts the (already lower-cased) domain and throws `DisposableEmailDomainException` if it's blocklisted. Also updated the class Javadoc.\n- `DisposableEmailDomainException.java` (new) \u2014 a checked escalation, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException`.\n- `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler.java` (new) \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, matching the existing email handler.\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DisposableEmailDomainException.yml` (new) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\nThe check runs inside the already-first `ValidateOwner` step and uses a dedicated exception rather than overloading `InvalidEmailException`, keeping the \"invalid syntax\" vs. \"blocked domain\" semantics distinct while both correctly returning 400.\n\nTest result: `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` passes \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
